{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 42, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was born around 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life and Regency* Raymond III was still a minor when his father was assassinated in 1152 and his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. After Hodierna's regency ended Raymond III took control of the County of Tripoli.\n\n*Reign and Military Campaigns* Raymond III's reign was marked by a series of conflicts with neighboring states and internal power struggles. In 1160 he was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi and held for nearly ten years. During his captivity the County of Tripoli was governed by a regent. After his release in 1174 Raymond III returned to Tripoli and resumed control of the county. He then formed an alliance with the Principality of Antioch and the Kingdom of Cilicia to counter the growing power of the Zengid dynasty. In 1180 Raymond III was appointed regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem on behalf of King Baldwin IV who was suffering from leprosy.\n\n*Succession Crisis and Death* Raymond III's regency in Jerusalem was marked by a bitter power struggle between him and Guy of Lusignan the husband of Baldwin's sister Sibylla. The struggle ultimately led to Raymond III's downfall and the rise of Guy of Lusignan to the throne. In 1187 Raymond III died without an heir and the County of Tripoli passed to his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III Prince of Antioch. Raymond III's death marked the end of an era in the County of Tripoli and had significant implications for the future of the Crusader states. Throughout his life Raymond III was known for his military prowess and his diplomatic skills and played a significant role in shaping the history of the Crusader states in the 12th century.